  <h2>Read the public record before debating the proposal</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Fictional Riverton market access file</h3>
    <p>Over twelve baseline weekends, Riverton's transportation department recorded seven reported vehicle-pedestrian near misses and two minor-injury crashes on four streets around Central Market. Two roadside sensors recorded an average nitrogen-dioxide concentration of 31 micrograms per cubic meter during market hours. The file provides no legal or health threshold for that number, and reported near misses may not include every event.</p>
    <p>The city received 184 voluntary consultation responses. Of these, 127 supported testing a weekend vehicle restriction. Forty-two respondents identified themselves as shop owners, and 31 of those 42 raised concerns about older customers, mobility access, or deliveries. Because participation was voluntary, these figures describe respondents, not all residents or all shop owners.</p>
    <p>The council authorized a three-month pilot on four streets, from 10:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m. on Saturdays and Sundays. Emergency vehicles and registered mobility services remain exempt. Deliveries may enter before 9:30 a.m. or after 4:30 p.m., with permits for documented time-sensitive needs. The budget allocates $72,000 for signs, staff, independent monitoring, and an accessible-trip support line.</p>
    <p>An independent evaluator will report pedestrian counts, vehicle counts, nitrogen dioxide, bus travel time, delivery completion, accessible-trip requests, access complaints, and voluntary aggregated business footfall. The council will review the pilot after six weeks and at its end. It may revise or suspend an access arrangement if evidence shows serious harm. The decision does not authorize a permanent restriction or claim that the trial will solve every safety, air-quality, access, or business issue.</p>
  </div>
